Conductive Hearing Loss
Opthalmology & ENT
Decreased sound perception, Weber lateralizing to affected ear, Bone conduction > air conduction

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-013
Reason for Visit: Sudden, distressing unilateral deafness accompanied by severe aural fullness and mild imbalance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden-onset unilateral hearing loss and aural fullness. This presentation mandated an urgent after-hours physical assessment to differentiate a benign, easily reversible conductive cause (like severe cerumen impaction) from Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL), which is an otologic emergency requiring high-dose corticosteroids within 24-48 hours to prevent permanent, irreversible deafness.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right ear completely blocked up after my shower, everything sounds muffled, and my own voice is booming inside my head."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute, dramatic decrease in the perception of sound intensity in the right ear occurring 3 hours ago immediately following a hot shower. Describes profound aural fullness and a physical sensation of blockage. Notes an unusually quiet speaking voice because the bone-conduction perception of their own voice is amplified on the right side. Endorses Paracusis Willisii, noting they can hear the spouse better when the television is turned up loud. Denies true objective vertigo, severe otalgia, purulent discharge, or recent head trauma.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly anxious and disoriented due to asymmetric hearing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is distressed, fearing a stroke or permanent deafness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, tilting head to the left, frequently pulling at the right pinna.
Physical Exam: Otoscopy of the left ear reveals a normal, pearly grey tympanic membrane with a sharp cone of light. Otoscopy of the right ear reveals complete obstruction of the external auditory canal by a dense, dark, impacted cerumen plug tightly adhered to the canal walls; the tympanic membrane cannot be visualized. Tuning fork tests (512 Hz): Weber test lateralizes distinctly to the affected right ear. Rinne test reveals bone conduction is greater than air conduction (BC > AC) on the right, confirming a conductive deficit. Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves and normal cerebellar function (no nystagmus or ataxia).
Environmental Observations: Cotton buds visible on the bathroom counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Conductive Hearing Loss secondary to Cerumen Impaction - Status: Acute, stable
Differentials:

Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Weber lateralizing to the affected ear and Rinne showing BC > AC (confirming conductive, not sensorineural etiology), plus direct visualization of total mechanical obstruction.

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, severe throbbing otalgia, and preceding upper respiratory tract infection.

Tympanic Membrane Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete obscuration of the TM by wax, but no history of acute barotrauma, explosive noise, or bloody/purulent discharge.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a sudden, highly distressing unilateral hearing loss, a symptom that requires immediate evaluation to rule out the otologic emergency of sudden sensorineural hearing loss. Urgent physical examination and tuning fork testing definitively diagnosed an acute conductive hearing loss, while otoscopy visually confirmed massive cerumen impaction, likely expanded by recent water exposure. By definitively ruling out sensorineural pathology, the urgent visit eliminated the need for emergency department transfer, high-dose systemic steroids, and extreme patient anxiety. Immediate management focused on softening the impaction for future safe removal, resolving the immediate clinical crisis safely in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Cerumol (chlorobutanol/arachis oil) ear drops, 5 drops to the right ear twice daily for 3 days to soften the impacted wax.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate the softening process and prevent iatrogenic injury, the patient must strictly lie on their side for 10 minutes after applying the drops. The gravity-assisted penetration of the oil will eventually cause the wax to spontaneously shift or fall out, providing intense, immediate relief of the blockage (positive reinforcement) and preventing the dangerous habit of blind cotton-bud instrumentation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for definitive ear syringing or micro-suction once the cerumen is sufficiently softened.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops severe, spinning vertigo, intractable vomiting, severe worsening pain, or purulent fluid draining from the ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and anatomical; tympanic membrane cannot be evaluated until wax is cleared.
Clinical Course: Hearing will remain muffled and may temporarily worsen as the oil expands the wax before it softens. Full recovery of hearing is expected upon removal.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to dig the wax out. If you suddenly become extremely dizzy, start vomiting, or develop severe pain in the ear tonight, present to the emergency department.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A large plug of wax has absorbed water from your shower, expanded, and completely blocked your ear canal. The drops will slowly melt the wax so we can safely flush it out later this week.
Actionable Steps: Instill 5 drops of the wax softener, then lie on your opposite side for 10 minutes. Throw away all cotton buds immediately, as they simply ram the wax deeper against your eardrum.

